[s0l0!-header][h1]switch[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

A [b]switch[/b] statement is a shorter way of writing a sequence of [b]if/else[/b] statements.

For example, imagine having the following [b]if/else[/b] statements:[code format="go" codeId="3315"]num := 3
 
if num == 1 {
  fmt.Println("One")
} else if num == 2 {
  fmt.Println("Two")
} else  if num == 3 {
  fmt.Println("Three")
} else {
  fmt.Println(num)
}[/code]
The code checks the value of the [b]num[/b] variable and outputs the corresponding text version.

We can achieve the same result using a [b]switch[/b] statement:
[code format="go" codeId="3316"]num := 3

switch num {
case 1:
  fmt.Println("One")
case 2:
  fmt.Println("Two")
case 3:
  fmt.Println("Three")
default:
   fmt.Println(num)
} [/code]
The switch statement makes the code shorter and easier to read.

Each [b]case[/b] statement includes the value to compare with and the code to run after the colon :. 

A simple way to replace an [b]if/else[/b] chain is to use a [b]switch[/b] without an expression. That way, each [b]case[/b] statement can include a condition:[code format="go" codeId="3317"]x := 2

 switch {
  case x>0 && x<10:
    fmt.Println("something")
  case x > 10:
    fmt.Println("something else")
}[/code]
[note]The optional [b]default[/b] case runs if none of the cases match.[/note]

